--- /dev/null
+++ b/uqmid/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,1162 @@
+uqmid - QMI modem daemon with a ModemManager style ubus API
+============================================================
+
+uqmid owns the QMI control port of a modem (`/dev/wwan0qmi0` of the
+wwan subsystem, or `/dev/cdc-wdm0` of usbmisc), keeps the services and client
+ids and exposes the modem over ubus. Its design follows ModemManager (MM):
+the same modem state model, the same enable/disable/power semantics, the
+same object model (one ubus object per modem, later per bearer, sim, sms and
+call) and the same way to learn about devices (kernel events are reported to
+the daemon, like `mmcli --report-kernel-event`).
+
+This file documents what is implemented so far. Names (state, power state,
+failure and change reasons) are the strings of ModemManager.
+
+Objects
+-------
+
+| ubus object        | MM counterpart                                   |
+|--------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
+| `uqmid`            | `org.freedesktop.ModemManager1` (the manager)    |
+| `uqmid.modem.<N>`  | `.../Modem/<N>` (Modem interface)                |
+| `uqmid.modem.<N>.sim.<M>` | `.../SIM/<M>` (Sim interface)             |
+| `uqmid.modem.<N>.bearer.<M>` | `.../Bearer/<M>` (Bearer interface)    |
+| `uqmid.modem.<N>.call.<M>` | `.../Call/<M>` (Call interface)          |
+| `uqmid.modem.<N>.sms.<M>` | `.../SMS/<M>` (Sms interface)             |
+
+The modem object also carries the Modem3gpp (registration, `register`, `scan`,
+initial EPS bearer settings), the Modem3gppProfileManager (`profile_list`, `profile_set`, `profile_delete`), the Voice (`voice_*` methods, calls), the Messaging (`messaging_*` methods, SMS)
+the Signal (`signal_*` methods, extended signal information) and the Simple (`simple_connect`, ...) interfaces of the same MM object.
+
+`N` is an index which is incremented for every modem added during the
+lifetime of the daemon (it is never reused), like MM's `/Modem/N`. `M` of the
+sim, bearer and call objects is counted the same way (per daemon lifetime).
+
+Manager `uqmid`
+---------------
+
+| method | arguments | result | MM |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `list` | - | `{"modems": [{path, index, device, port, physdev, subsystem, state}]}` | `GetManagedObjects` / `mmcli -L` |
+| `report_kernel_event` | `{action: "add"\|"remove", subsystem, name, sysfs?}` | - | `ReportKernelEvent` |
+| `scan_devices` | - | `{found, added}` | `ScanDevices` (here: look for `/dev/wwan*qmi*` and `/dev/cdc-wdm*`) |
+
+`report_kernel_event` handles the subsystems `wwan` (name `wwan0qmi0`) and
+`usbmisc` (name `cdc-wdm0`). Other ports of the wwan subsystem (AT, MBIM) are
+ignored. `net` events only refresh the list of paired net ports. A modem
+which is already known is not added a second time, so the cached events of
+the hotplug scripts can be replayed at any time.
+
+Notifications (`ubus subscribe uqmid`):
+
+* `modem.added {path, device}`
+* `modem.removed {path, device}`
+
+Modem `uqmid.modem.<N>`
+-----------------------
+
+### Properties: `get`
+
+All properties are returned by `get` (snake_case names of the MM properties):
+
+| key | value |
+|---|---|
+| `path`, `index` | ubus object name and its index |
+| `state` | `failed`, `unknown`, `initializing`, `locked`, `disabled`, `disabling`, `enabling`, `enabled`, `searching`, `registered`, `disconnecting`, `connecting`, `connected` |
+| `state_failed_reason` | `none`, `unknown`, `sim-missing`, `sim-error`, `unknown-capabilities` (not `none` only in the `failed` state) |
+| `power_state` | `unknown`, `off`, `low`, `on` |
+| `unlock_required` | `none`, `sim-pin`, `sim-puk`, `unknown` (`unknown` also while initializing) |
+| `unlock_retries` | `{"sim-pin": n, "sim-puk": n}` from the UIM card status, empty if unknown |
+| `manufacturer`, `model`, `revision`, `imei`, `imeisv`, `meid`, `equipment_identifier` | from DMS |
+| `device` | sysfs path of the physical device (MM `Device`/physdev; the parent platform device for wwan ports, the usb device for cdc-wdm) |
+| `plugin`, `drivers` | `uqmid` and the kernel drivers of the net ports |
+| `primary_port`, `device_node`, `ports` | control port, `/dev` node and all ports `[{name, type: qmi\|net}]` |
+| `net_ports` | `[{ifname, driver, dev_port}]` the paired netdevs. `dev_port` of a bam-dmux netdev selects the QMI SIO data port `A2_MUX_RMNET<dev_port>` |
+| `sim` | ubus path of the sim object (`uqmid.modem.<N>.sim.<M>`), `"/"` if there is no SIM |
+| `sim_slots` | paths of the sim objects per physical slot, `"/"` for an empty or inactive slot. If the modem does not support the UIM slot status (e.g. the MSM8916 SoC modem answers 94/Not supported) there is one entry (deviation: MM leaves the list empty) |
+| `primary_sim_slot` | number of the active slot (from 1), 0 if unknown |
+| `carrier_configuration` | MM `CarrierConfiguration`: description of the active PDC SOFTWARE configuration, `default` if none is selected, empty if PDC is not available or failed |
+| `own_numbers` | MM `OwnNumbers`: array of strings, DMS Get MSISDN (at most one entry). Loaded once when the SIM becomes ready (MM `INITIALIZATION_STEP_OWN_NUMBERS`, also after an unlock); not loaded again once it succeeded. Empty if the SIM/modem reports no number or the request failed (retried after the next SIM ready) |
+| `current_capabilities`, `supported_capabilities`, `supported_modes`, `current_modes`, `supported_bands`, `current_bands` | MM `CurrentCapabilities`, `SupportedCapabilities`, `SupportedModes`, `CurrentModes`, `SupportedBands`, `CurrentBands`, see "Modes and bands" |
+| `profile_manager_index_field` | MM `Modem3gppProfileManager.IndexField` (`profile_id`), only if the profile manager is supported, see "3GPP profile manager" |
+| `registration_state`, `operator_code`, `operator_name`, `access_technologies`, `signal_quality`, `initial_eps_bearer_settings` | the Modem3gpp properties, see "3GPP interface" |
+| `bearers` | paths of the bearer objects |
+| `calls` | paths of the call objects (newest first), terminated calls stay until they are deleted |
+| `messages` | paths of the sms objects (newest first), see "Messaging interface" |
+| `supported_storages` | `["sm", "me"]` (the SIM and the modem memory, MM `SupportedStorages`) |
+| `default_storage` | MM `DefaultStorage`: the storage of the received messages, `unknown` until the modem was enabled once |
+| `emergency_only` | MM `EmergencyOnly`: `true` while the modem state is below `registered`, only emergency calls are allowed then |
+| `carrier_configuration_revision` | MM `CarrierConfigurationRevision`: version of that configuration as `%08X`, empty if unknown |
+
+### Net ports
+
+* wwan subsystem: all netdevs whose sysfs device is below the parent device
+  of the wwan port (`/sys/class/wwan/<port>/../device`), the same pairing as
+  MM's qcom-soc plugin and the netifd `qmi.sh`.
+* usbmisc: the netdev below the usb interface of the cdc-wdm port.
+
+uqmid does not touch `raw_ip` or other sysfs attributes: bam-dmux netdevs
+are raw-ip already.
+
+### Methods
+
+All methods which change the state are executed one after the other per
+modem (MM: operation lock). The state is checked when the request gets its
+turn. A failed request returns an error status and the reply
+`{"error": <name>, "message": <text>}`, the error names are `failed`,
+`wrong-state`, `invalid-args`, `unsupported`, `aborted`, `unauthorized`, `in-progress`, `not-found`
+(MM core errors) and `network-not-allowed` / `network-timeout` (MM mobile equipment errors of a failed
+registration).
+The ubus status of a failed request is a standard `UBUS_STATUS_*` code:
+
+| error | ubus status |
+|---|---|
+| `wrong-state` | `PERMISSION_DENIED` |
+| `invalid-args` | `INVALID_ARGUMENT` |
+| `unsupported` | `NOT_SUPPORTED` |
+| `timeout` | `TIMEOUT` |
+| `aborted` (the modem went away), `not-found` | `NOT_FOUND` |
+| `unauthorized`, `network-not-allowed` | `PERMISSION_DENIED` |
+| `network-timeout` | `TIMEOUT` |
+| `failed`, `in-progress` | `UNKNOWN_ERROR` |
+
+A successful request returns `{"state", "power_state"}`.
+
+* `enable {enable: bool}` (MM: `Enable`)
+  * `enable: true`
+    * below `disabled` (failed, unknown, initializing, locked): error `wrong-state`
+    * already `enabled` (or beyond): success, nothing is done
+    * `disabled` -> `enabling` -> power up (DMS operating mode online; if the
+      modem answers INTERNAL or INVALID_TRANSITION the update is retried once)
+      -> `enabled`. If powering up fails the state goes back to `disabled`
+      (reason `failure`).
+    * then the 3gpp interface is enabled (NAS indications, initial EPS bearer
+      settings, a first registration check, see "3GPP interface"). The state
+      after the enable is `searching` or `registered` if the registration
+      subsystem says so, `enabled` otherwise (MM: `update_state_internal()`).
+    * then the messaging interface is enabled (default storage, the SMS of the SIM and the modem are loaded,
+      the event report is switched on, see "Messaging interface"). This step is placed after 3gpp and before voice
+      like in MM and never fails the enable.
+    * then the voice interface is enabled (see "Voice interface").
+  * `enable: false`
+    * `disabled` or `locked`: success, nothing is done
+    * `enabled` (or beyond) -> `disabling` -> disconnect all bearers (a bearer which
+      is connecting is cancelled) -> disable voice (call indications are unregistered) ->
+      disable messaging (the event report is switched off, the WMS client is kept, the SMS objects are
+      removed) -> disable 3gpp (indications are switched off, the registration state is
+      `unknown` again) -> `disabled`. The persistent QMI clients remain allocated until the modem is removed;
+      the radio is NOT powered down.
+* `set_power_state {state: "on"|"low"|"off"}` (MM: `SetPowerState`)
+  * the cached power state is refreshed from the modem first; nothing is done
+    if it already is the requested one
+  * updates are at least 2 seconds apart
+  * `low` is allowed in every state; if the modem is above `disabled` it is
+    disabled afterwards (also if the power update failed)
+  * `on` is only allowed in `disabled` and `failed`
+  * `off` is only allowed in `locked`, `disabled` and `failed`
+  * `low` is DMS low_power, `on` online and `off` offline
+* `reset` (MM: `Reset`, mm-shared-qmi.c): DMS set operating mode `offline`,
+  then `reset` (20 s timeout each). Success is returned once the modem
+  accepted the reset. The modem vanishes afterwards (the ports are removed and
+  added again by the kernel, hotplug re-adds the modem object).
+
+Notifications of the modem object (`ubus subscribe uqmid.modem.<N>`):
+
+* `state_changed {old, new, reason, failed_reason?}`: `reason` is `unknown`,
+  `user-requested`, `suspend` or `failure`. `failed_reason` is only present
+  if the new state is `failed`.
+* `power_state_changed {old, new}`
+* `registration_state_changed {old, new, operator_code, operator_name}`
+* `bearer.added {path}`, `bearer.removed {path}`, `bearer.connected {path, interface}` and
+  `bearer.disconnected {path, interface, connection_error?}`
+* `voice.call_added {path}`, `voice.call_deleted {path}` and
+  `voice.call_state_changed {path, old, new, reason, end_reason?, cc_cause?}` (the last one is an uqmid addition: the same as
+  `state_changed` of the call object, for a single subscription on the modem object)
+* `signal.updated {rate, rssi_threshold, error_rate_threshold, cdma?, evdo?, gsm?, umts?, lte?, nr5g?}`
+  (see "Signal interface")
+* `messaging.added {path, received}` and `messaging.deleted {path}` (MM: `Added` / `Deleted`) and
+  `messaging.state_changed {path, old, new}` (an uqmid addition: the same as `state_changed` of the sms object)
+
+3GPP interface (Modem3gpp)
+--------------------------
+
+Properties (in `get` of the modem object, MM names in snake_case):
+
+| key | value |
+|---|---|
+| `registration_state` | `idle`, `home`, `searching`, `denied`, `unknown`, `roaming`, `home-sms-only`, `roaming-sms-only`, `emergency-only`, `home-csfb-not-preferred`, `roaming-csfb-not-preferred`, `attached-rlos` |
+| `operator_code` | MCC+MNC of the network (empty if not registered) |
+| `operator_name` | description of the network (empty if not registered) |
+| `access_technologies` | array of `gsm`, `gprs`, `edge`, `umts`, `hsdpa`, ... `lte` (`unknown` if none). MM has a bitmask here |
+| `signal_quality` | `{value: 0-100, recent: bool}` |
+| `initial_eps_bearer_settings` | `{apn, ip_type, allowed_auth, user, profile_id}` of the default attach profile (the password is not shown), empty if it is not known |
+
+Registration state tracking (mm-broadband-modem-qmi.c, mm-iface-modem-3gpp.c):
+
+* While the modem is enabled the NAS service reports the state. The system
+  info indications are registered (the serving system events are turned off,
+  as MM does); if the modem does not support them the serving system
+  indications are used, and if that fails too the state is polled every 30 s.
+  `get system info` (fallback `get serving system`) gives the state when the
+  modem is enabled and for the registration checks of `register`.
+* CS, PS and EPS states are consolidated like `get_consolidated_reg_state()`
+  (home/roaming first, then searching, denied, emergency only, idle).
+* The modem state follows: not registered -> `enabled`, searching ->
+  `searching`, registered -> `registered` (the operator code and name are loaded
+  first). While the modem is connecting, connected or disconnecting
+  the registration does not change the modem state (`update_state_internal()`). MM does not debounce registration
+  states: consolidated NAS updates are reported immediately, so weak-signal home/searching transitions can flap.
+* The signal quality (`MM_RSSI_TO_QUALITY()`: -113 dBm 0%, -51 dBm 100%) is queried once on enable/registration
+  (Get Signal Info, fallback Get Signal Strength) and comes from signal info indications (fallback: signal strength
+  event report with MM thresholds). Like MM's initial signal check, a 0%/invalid result is polled every 3 s up to
+  five times; after that it is refreshed every 30 s only if the modem sends no indications.
+
+Methods:
+
+* `register {operator_id?}` (MM: `Register`, `mm_iface_modem_3gpp_register_in_network()`):
+  an empty or missing `operator_id` is the automatic registration, otherwise
+  `<MCC><MNC>` (5 or 6 digits). The modem state has to be `enabled`, `searching`
+  or `registered` (`wrong-state` otherwise). The registration state is checked
+  first and nothing is done if the modem is registered as requested. Then the
+  NAS `initiate network register` is sent (fallback: set system selection
+  preference) and the state is checked every 3 s until the modem is registered,
+  the registration is `denied` (`network-not-allowed`) or 60 s passed
+  (`network-timeout`). A failed registration sets the states to `idle`, like MM.
+  A new `register` aborts the one which is running. Reply: `{registration_state,
+  operator_code, operator_name}`. Not serialized with the other operations (MM
+  does not lock either).
+* `scan` (MM: `Scan`, `mm_shared_qmi_3gpp_scan_networks()`): NAS network scan
+  (GSM, UMTS and LTE, 300 s timeout), the modem has to be enabled. Reply:
+  `{networks: [{operator_code, status: current|available|forbidden|unknown, access_technology: [...],
+  operator_long?}]}`. The 5GNR scan is not done.
+* `set_initial_eps_bearer_settings {apn?, ip_type?, allowed_auth?, user?, password?, force?}`
+  (MM: `SetInitialEpsBearerSettings`, `handle_set_initial_eps_bearer_settings_step()`): serialized with
+  the other state changing requests. Nothing is done if the settings are the
+  current ones (unless `force`). Otherwise: power the modem down to `low`
+  (power update of the modem object, no disable), modify the default attach
+  profile (WDS get LTE attach PDN list gives the profile index; WDS modify
+  profile), restore the previous power state and read the profile again to
+  check it. Deviation: if the modem does not know the attach PDN list the
+  profile 1 is used (MM fails). The result is `{state, power_state}`.
+
+Signal interface (Modem.Signal)
+-------------------------------
+
+Extended signal information of the modem object (MM: `mm-iface-modem-signal.c`, with the QMI parts of
+`mm-broadband-modem-qmi.c`: "Load extended signal information", "Setup threshold values",
+`nas_signal_info_indication_cb()`, `common_process_signal_info()`). The interface exists if the modem has the QMI
+NAS service (MM: `check_support()`), otherwise the methods fail with `unsupported`.
+
+Methods (the modem has to be at least `disabled`, `wrong-state` otherwise, like
+`mm_iface_modem_abort_invocation_if_state_not_reached()`):
+
+* `signal_setup {rate}` (MM: `Setup`): refresh rate in seconds, 0 disables the periodic refresh. The values are
+  loaded at once and then every `rate` seconds while the modem is enabled (`polling_restart()`).
+* `signal_setup_thresholds {rssi_threshold, error_rate_threshold}` (MM: `SetupThresholds`): both optional (missing
+  is 0 / false, as in MM's dictionary parser). `rssi_threshold` is the RSSI delta in dBm which triggers a signal info
+  indication (0: default 5 dBm): NAS Config Signal Info v2 with that delta for CDMA, HDR, GSM, WCDMA, LTE RSSI and
+  5G NR RSRP; if the modem answers InvalidQmiCommand / NotSupported the v1 message with a list of RSSI thresholds
+  from -125 dBm in steps of the threshold up to -30 dBm (default list without a threshold). The QMI implementation
+  of MM does not support the error rate threshold: `error_rate_threshold: true` fails with `unsupported`. The
+  thresholds are only kept if the modem accepted them, setting the current values again is a no-op. A query is
+  made right away after a successful setup.
+* `signal_get` -> `{rate, rssi_threshold, error_rate_threshold, cdma?, evdo?, gsm?, umts?, lte?, nr5g?}` (MM
+  properties `Rate`, `Threshold`, `ErrorRateThreshold`, `Cdma`, `Evdo`, `Gsm`, `Umts`, `Lte`, `Nr5g`).
+
+Value representation: a technology is a table with the values (doubles). MM's empty dictionary for "no values of
+that technology" is a missing key here, and a value which is unknown (`MM_SIGNAL_UNKNOWN`) is left out of the table
+(as `mm_signal_get_dictionary()` does). Keys: `rssi` (dBm), `rscp` (dBm), `ecio` (dB), `sinr` (dB), `io` (dBm),
+`rsrp` (dBm), `rsrq` (dB), `snr` (dB) and `error_rate`. Which ones are present (MM): cdma `rssi, ecio`; evdo `rssi,
+ecio, sinr, io`; gsm `rssi`; umts `rssi, ecio, rscp`; lte `rssi, rsrq, rsrp, snr`; nr5g `rsrp, snr, rsrq`.
+`error_rate` is never set by the QMI implementation.
+
+Loading (MM: `signal_load_values()`): NAS Get Signal Info (5 s); if it gives no technology the Get Signal Strength
+(request mask RSSI, ECIO, IO, SINR, RSRQ, LTE SNR, LTE RSRP, 5 s). Conversions: ECIO is the QMI value times -0.5
+dB, SNR the QMI value (units of 0.1 dB) times 0.1, WCDMA RSCP the QMI value times -1, the EV-DO SINR level is
+mapped to dB (-9 .. +9). Signal info indications (registered by the 3gpp interface) update the values the same way.
+A failure to load is only logged.
+
+Gating (MM: `common_enable_disable()`, `mm_iface_modem_signal_update()`): the interface is enabled after the
+messaging interface and before the voice interface of an `enable`, and disabled after voice on `enable: false`.
+Enabling and disabling reset the values, start/stop the polling and (re)configure the thresholds (never fails the
+enable). Values are only published (and `signal_get` only has technologies) while the modem is enabled and the
+rate or a threshold is not 0; otherwise they are reset. The rate and thresholds are kept over a disable.
+
+Notification of the modem object: `signal.updated {rate, rssi_threshold, error_rate_threshold, cdma?, ...}` (the
+content of `signal_get`), sent when a property changed (MM: `PropertiesChanged` of the Signal interface, which is
+only emitted for changed values).
+
+Deviations: the RSSI of the Get Signal Strength RSSI list is a magnitude (-dBm) in QMI and is negated (MM reports
+it unsigned); 5G values of -32768 (QMI: not connected) are not reported; the query after the thresholds setup is not
+made while the interface is disabled.
+
+Cell info
+---------
+
+`get_cell_info` of the modem object (MM: `Modem.GetCellInfo`, `handle_get_cell_info()` in `mm-iface-modem.c` and
+`get_cell_info()` / `get_cell_info_ready()` of `mm-broadband-modem-qmi.c`, MM 1.24.2) -> `{cells: [...]}`. The
+modem has to be at least `enabled` (`wrong-state` otherwise, like `mm_iface_modem_abort_invocation_if_state_not_reached()`),
+`unsupported` without the NAS service. It sends NAS Get Cell Location Info (10 s timeout); a failure is replied as
+`failed` / `timeout`.
+
+Every cell is a table `{cell_type, serving, ...}` as `mm_cell_info_get_dictionary()` builds it: `cell_type` is a
+string (`gsm`, `umts`, `lte`, `nr5g`, `cdma`, `tdscdma`) instead of the MM number, `serving` is always present, and
+the other keys are the MM property names with `-` replaced by `_`. A property MM did not set is left out.
+
+| cell_type | keys |
+|---|---|
+| `gsm` | `operator_id`, `lac`, `ci`, `timing_advance`, `arfcn`, `base_station_id`, `rx_level` |
+| `lte` | `operator_id`, `tac`, `ci`, `physical_ci`, `earfcn`, `rsrp`, `rsrq`, `timing_advance` |
+| `nr5g` | `operator_id`, `tac`, `ci`, `physical_ci`, `nrarfcn`, `rsrp`, `rsrq`, `sinr` |
+
+`operator_id` is the PLMN (BCD, filler skipped); `lac`, `tac`, `ci`, `physical_ci` and `base_station_id` are
+upper-case hexadecimal strings without leading zeros; `rsrp`, `rsrq` and `sinr` are doubles (QMI value times 0.1);
+`timing_advance`, `arfcn`, `rx_level`, `earfcn` and `nrarfcn` are numbers. MM's QMI parser (copied as it is) produces:
+the serving GSM cell and the GSM neighbours (the neighbours get the timing advance of the serving cell), the
+intra-frequency LTE cells (serving where the physical cell id is the serving cell id; only the serving one has `ci`
+and `timing_advance`), the inter-frequency LTE neighbours (`earfcn`, `physical_ci`, `rsrp`, `rsrq` only) and the NR5G
+serving cell. UMTS, CDMA, TD-SCDMA and the other neighbour lists are not reported by MM 1.24.2 and therefore not here
+either.
+
+Modes and bands
+---------------
+
+The modem object has the MM properties `SupportedModes`, `CurrentModes`, `SupportedBands`, `CurrentBands` and the
+methods `SetCurrentModes` and `SetCurrentBands` (`mm-iface-modem.c` and `mm-shared-qmi.c` of MM 1.24.2, the
+translations are those of `mm-modem-helpers-qmi.c`). The MM values are bit masks and numbers, here they are the nicks
+of the MM enums:
+
+* modes: `cs`, `2g`, `3g`, `4g`, `5g` and `any` (`MMModemMode`). `supported_modes` is `[{allowed: [...], preferred: "..."}]`,
+  `current_modes` is `{allowed: [...], preferred: "..."}`; `allowed` is a list of nicks (`["any"]` for the
+  all-bits value), `preferred` is one nick or `none`.
+* bands: the nicks of `MMModemBand` (`egsm`, `dcs`, `pcs`, `g850`, `utran-1`, `eutran-1`, `ngran-78`, `cdma-bc0`,
+  `any`, `unknown`, ...), `supported_bands` and `current_bands` are sorted like in MM. A band number without a nick in
+  the MM enum (e.g. EUTRAN 15) is not listed.
+
+Defaults (the MM skeleton values while nothing was loaded): supported modes `[{allowed: ["any"], preferred: "none"}]`,
+current modes `{allowed: ["any"], preferred: "none"}`, bands `["unknown"]`.
+
+### Loading
+
+The steps of `interface_initialization_step()` of MM run as steps of the initialization of the modem, in the order of
+MM: the current capabilities (`load_current_capabilities`: NAS Get System Selection Preference, NAS Get Technology
+Preference, DMS Get Capabilities, which give the features SSP / TP / extended LTE bands / acquisition order and the
+radio interfaces) and the supported capabilities right after the version info, then (after the identity of the modem)
+the supported modes (built from the radio interfaces and the features by
+`mm_supported_modes_from_qmi_supported_modes_context()`, so there are modes with a preferred mode only if NAS SSP is
+available) and the supported bands (DMS Get Band Capabilities, extended LTE and NR5G band lists when present), then
+(after the power state) the current modes (NAS Get System Selection Preference, or NAS Get Technology Preference if SSP
+is not supported; the preferred mode comes from the acquisition order preference or, for 2G+3G only, from the
+GSM/WCDMA acquisition order) and the current bands (NAS Get System Selection Preference: band preference, LTE or
+extended LTE band preference, the union of the NR5G SA and NSA band preferences; filtered by the supported bands), then
+the SIM steps. All requests have a 5 s timeout. If there is one supported mode combination only the current modes are
+that one without a request. Everything which is loaded is not loaded again ("meant to be loaded only once").
+
+Failures like in MM:
+
+* DMS Get Capabilities fails, the modem has no current capabilities, or the NAS or DMS client is missing: the
+  initialization fails (`mm-iface-modem.c` `load_current_capabilities_ready()` makes it `MM_CORE_ERROR_UNSUPPORTED`,
+  `mm-broadband-modem.c` `iface_modem_initialize_ready()` turns that into the state `failed` with the failed reason
+  `unknown-capabilities`); the later steps are not run. The method `set_current_capabilities` still works in that state.
+* NAS SSP and NAS TP both fail: not an error. Both features stay unsupported, they are not asked again (a modem in low
+  power mode may answer neither: then no modes can be set and the supported modes are the one combination of all
+  the radio interfaces).
+* The failure of any other step is only logged (the property keeps its default).
+
+After an unlock (MM runs the initialization steps again) the supported and current modes and bands steps run again for
+what failed before; a set request which gets its turn meanwhile waits for them.
+
+Deviations from MM: a MM band without a nick in the MM enum (e.g. EUTRAN 15) is not listed (MM prints the number; it
+is still used internally), and "an extended LTE/NR5G band list is reported" is "the list is not empty" (the QMI
+parser does not tell an empty TLV from a missing one). The NR5G band TLVs are used as in MM.
+
+### Capabilities
+
+* `current_capabilities`: array of the nicks of `MMModemCapability` (`pots`, `cdma-evdo`, `gsm-umts`, `lte`,
+  `lte-advanced`, `iridium`, `5gnr`, `tds`; `["none"]` until they are loaded).
+* `supported_capabilities`: array of the combinations, each an array of nicks (empty until they are loaded), as
+  `mm_supported_capabilities_from_qmi_supported_capabilities_context()` builds them: the full set of the radio
+  interfaces, and for a GSM/UMTS+CDMA/EVDO multimode modem with NAS SSP or TP also GSM/UMTS+rest, CDMA/EVDO+rest and the
+  rest alone. A modem like the MSM8916 one has exactly one combination.
+* `set_current_capabilities {capabilities: ["gsm-umts", "lte"]}` (MM: `SetCurrentCapabilities`, implemented by
+  `mm_shared_qmi_set_current_capabilities()`; serialized by the operation lock, but allowed in every state: "Setting
+  capabilities allowed also in FAILED state"). Nothing is done if they are the current ones. `unsupported` if only one
+  combination is supported (`Cannot change capabilities: only one combination supported`), if the combination is not
+  one of the supported ones, or if neither SSP nor TP is supported. NAS Set System Selection Preference (mode
+  preference, permanent; or NAS Set Technology Preference, where `no-effect` ends the operation without a reset), then,
+  like MM, the modem is reset (DMS offline, then reset: the reply is sent once the modem accepted the reset or when it
+  went away) and the modem object goes away: the device appears again after the reboot and is probed again
+  (`modem.removed`, `modem.added` with a new index; MM disposes the object the same way). Errors of the requests: `failed` /
+  `timeout`.
+
+### Methods
+
+Both are executed through the serialized operations of the modem (MM: the operation lock) and need at least the state
+`disabled` (`mm_iface_modem_abort_invocation_if_state_not_reached(DISABLED)`, error `wrong-state` with the message
+`modem in <state> state`). They return the usual reply (`{"state", "power_state"}`). After a successful set the signal
+polling is restarted (`mm_iface_modem_refresh_signal()`).
+
+* `set_current_modes {allowed: ["3g", "4g"], preferred: "4g"}` (MM: `SetCurrentModes`, `preferred` is optional and
+  `none` by default; `allowed: ["any"]`, `preferred: "none"` selects everything the modem has).
+  * `unsupported` if only one combination is supported (`Cannot change modes: only one combination supported`), if the
+    combination is not one of the supported ones (`any`/`none` is always accepted), or if `preferred` is not part of `allowed`;
+    nothing is sent if the modes already are the current ones.
+  * NAS Set System Selection Preference (5 s) with the change duration `permanent` and the mode preference. With a preferred
+    mode the acquisition order preference (if the modem reported one) and, if both 2G and 3G are allowed and the modem is 3GPP,
+    the GSM/WCDMA acquisition order preference are added. Without NAS SSP, NAS Set Technology Preference (duration
+    permanent; `no-effect` is ignored; a preferred mode is refused with `failed`). `allowed: ["any"]` becomes the union of
+    the 2G, 3G, 4G and 5G the modem supports and a given preferred mode is ignored (MM tests the not yet set
+    `ctx->preferred` in `mm_shared_qmi_set_current_modes()`). Neither SSP nor TP: `unsupported`.
+  * Then the current modes are loaded again, the first time right away, with up to 5 retries 1 second apart if the load
+    fails or the result differs. A load which keeps failing is not an error (the requested modes become the current ones),
+    a result which keeps differing is a `failed` error (`reloaded modes (allowed ... ) different to the requested ones
+    ...`) unless `any` was requested. The reloaded modes are the current modes in every case.
+* `set_current_bands {bands: ["eutran-3", "eutran-7"]}` (MM: `SetCurrentBands`; `["any"]` selects all supported bands;
+  an empty list is `["unknown"]` like in MM).
+  * Nothing is sent if the target list (`any` is the supported list) equals the current bands. A list of several bands must not
+    contain `any` or `unknown` and every band has to be supported (unless the supported bands are `any`/`unknown`):
+    `invalid-args`. A single band is not checked (as in MM). `any` with unknown supported bands is `failed`.
+  * NAS Set System Selection Preference (5 s), permanent: the band preference, for an LTE capable modem the extended LTE
+    band preference (or the LTE band preference if the modem did not report it) and always the NR5G SA and NSA band
+    preference with the same masks (MM does so also for modems without 5G).
+  * Then the current bands are loaded again, like the modes (5 retries, 1 s): a load which keeps failing makes the
+    requested bands the current ones, a different result is a `failed` error (`reloaded current bands (...) different to
+    the requested ones (...)`).
+
+Errors of the requests: `failed` with the QMI error (`QMI protocol error (N): 'name'`) or `timeout`. A malformed list
+is refused at once with `invalid-args` (`Unknown mode`, `Unknown band`) or the ubus status `INVALID_ARGUMENT`.
+
+3GPP profile manager
+--------------------
+
+The modem object has the Modem3gppProfileManager interface of MM (`mm-iface-modem-3gpp-profile-manager.c`, the QMI
+implementation of `mm-broadband-modem-qmi.c`, profile dictionary of `mm-3gpp-profile.c` of MM 1.24.2). The keys of a
+profile are the MM property names with `-` replaced by `_`:
+
+| key | value |
+|---|---|
+| `profile_id` | number (the WDS profile index, 1..255) |
+| `profile_name`, `apn`, `user`, `password` | strings (the password is in the dictionary like in MM, only the logs hide it) |
+| `ip_type` | `ipv4`, `ipv6`, `ipv4v6`, `non-ip` or `any` (a string, an array when read with several) |
+| `allowed_auth` | array of `none`, `pap`, `chap`, `mschap`, `mschapv2`, `eap` |
+| `apn_type` | array of `initial`, `default`, `ims`, `mms`, `management`, `voice`, `emergency`, `private`, `purchase`, `video-share`, `local`, `app`, `xcap`, `tethering` |
+| `access_type_preference` | `3gpp-only`, `3gpp-preferred`, `non-3gpp-only` |
+| `roaming_allowance` | array of `home`, `partner`, `non-partner` |
+| `profile_source` | `admin`, `user`, `operator`, `modem`, `device` |
+| `profile_enabled` | boolean (MM's key is `profile-enabled`) |
+| `pcscf_using_pco` | boolean, uqmid extension (MM has none): WDS "PCSCF Address Using PCO" (TLV 0x1F) of the profile |
+| `im_cn` | boolean, uqmid extension (MM has none): WDS "IMCN Flag" (TLV 0x22) of the profile |
+
+A key which MM leaves out of the dictionary (unknown / none / not set) is left out. QMI reports the id, the name, the
+APN, the IP type, the authentication, a non-empty user and password and the APN type (WDS Get Profile Settings); the
+access type preference, the roaming allowance, the source and the enabled flag are never reported and ignored when
+set. The uqmid extensions `pcscf_using_pco` and `im_cn` are reported only if the modem returns the TLV, and the TLV is
+sent by `profile_set` (create and modify) only if the key was given (a profile which already has the given value is not
+written again; a key which was not given is never compared and never sent).
+
+The interface is supported if the profile list could be loaded when the modem was initialized (once, when the SIM is
+ready, only for a 3GPP modem: MM's `INITIALIZATION_STEP_CHECK_SUPPORT`, without an explicit check for QMI). MM does not
+export the interface otherwise: the methods fail with `unsupported` (`Profile management not supported`). All
+methods need the state `enabled` or above (`wrong-state`, `modem in <state> state`) and, like in MM, are NOT put into the
+operation FIFO: MM only authorizes them (`mm_base_modem_authorize()`), so they may run next to other operations.
+
+* `profile_list` -> `{profiles: [...]}`: WDS Get Profile List (3GPP, 10 s) and WDS Get Profile Settings (3 s) of each
+  profile one after the other. As in MM one failing profile (or one with the internal `apn_disabled` flag: `Profile 'N' is
+  internally disabled`) fails the whole list (`Couldn't load settings from profile index N: ...`).
+* `profile_set {profile: {...}}` -> `{profile: {...}}` (MM: `Set`; the index field is `profile_id`, strict):
+  creates the profile if there is no `profile_id` (WDS Create Profile, the modem chooses the id), modifies the profile
+  with that id otherwise (WDS Modify Profile). Steps like `mm_iface_modem_3gpp_profile_manager_set_profile()`: the IP type
+  `none`/`any` becomes `ipv4`; the list of the profiles is read (a failure is only logged); with a `profile_id` the
+  profile has to exist (`not-found`, `Profile 'N' not found`) and is not written if it is equal to the requested one
+  (APN, IP type, id, authentication, user, password, APN type, enabled flag; MM compares the profile names by pointer, so
+  a profile with a name is never "equal"); a connected bearer which uses the profile fails the request
+  (`Cannot use profile N: found an already connected bearer`); the request is 10 s; an `InvalidProfileType` error of the
+  modem is retried once without the APN type (remembered; also when the settings of a profile have no APN type);
+  finally the stored profile is read again (`Couldn't validate update of profile 'N': ...`) and returned. The
+  authentication is CHAP if a user or password was given without `allowed_auth`, `none` otherwise
+  (`mm_bearer_allowed_auth_to_qmi_authentication()`; `unsupported` for a mask without PAP/CHAP/none), the APN type is
+  `default` if none was given, `non-ip` is refused (`failed`, `Invalid IP type specified`). Unknown keys are `invalid-args`.
+  Errors of the modem: `failed` (`Couldn't create profile: QMI protocol error (N): '...'`, with `DS profile error: <name>: `
+  in front for an extended error) or `timeout`.
+* `profile_delete {profile_id}` -> `{state, power_state}` (MM: `Delete`): a connected bearer which uses the profile fails
+  the request, then WDS Delete Profile (10 s).
+* Notification `profile_manager.updated {}` (MM: `Updated`) when the modem reports a profile change (WDS Profile Changed
+  indication) or a PDC refresh. Like in MM it is throttled (one per 2 seconds), and indications are ignored while a set or
+  delete of the user runs (and for 100 ms after a success). The indications are set up and enabled with the modem enable
+  (after the 3GPP interface, before messaging: PDC Register, WDS Indication Register for the profile changes, WDS
+  Configure Profile Event List for all profiles; a failure is only logged) and disabled with the disable.
+
+Deviations from MM: the `profile_id` of a request must be 1..255 (MM hands it to a `guint8` argument), a NULL string of
+the store requests (name, APN, user, password) is sent as an empty string (libqmi's behaviour for NULL is not known here),
+and a value of the wrong type is `invalid-args`.
+
+Bearers
+-------
+
+`create_bearer {apn, ip_type?, allowed_auth?, user?, password?, allow_roaming?, profile_id?}` (MM:
+`CreateBearer`) creates a bearer object and returns `{path}`. The modem has to be enabled. The `apn` property is
+required unless a `profile_id` is given (MM: "3GPP connection logic requires APN setting"), but it may be empty
+(the network default APN is used). `ip_type` is `ipv4`,
+`ipv6` or `ipv4v6` (none: IPv4 without an IP family preference, like MM's `any`), `allowed_auth` is
+`none`, `pap`, `chap` or an array of them (MM: mschap, mschapv2 and eap are not supported by QMI;
+without user and password `none` is used, with them `chap` if nothing was given, as MM). `allow_roaming` defaults to true (MM: `mm_bearer_properties_new()`); roaming is forbidden only if it is false. At most 8 bearers.
+`delete_bearer {path}` (MM: `DeleteBearer`) disconnects the bearer first.
+
+Bearer object `uqmid.modem.<N>.bearer.<M>`:
+
+* `get` (MM properties): `{path, index, modem, status: disconnected|connecting|connected|disconnecting,
+  interface, connected, suspended, connection_error?: {name, message}, ip4_config?, ip6_config?, stats:
+  {duration}, ip_timeout, bearer_type, properties: {apn, ip_type, allowed_auth?, user, allow_roaming,
+  profile_id?}}`. `interface` is the netdev of the data port while the bearer is not disconnected. The
+  `pcscf?: [address, ...]` and `pcscf_fqdn?: [name, ...]` (uqmid extensions, MM has no P-CSCF) are present while connected
+  and only if not empty: the P-CSCF addresses (IPv6 first, then IPv4, as text) and domain names of the connected families
+  (WDS Get Current Settings: P-CSCF address, server address list, IPv6 server address list and domain name list; at most
+  8 addresses and 4 names per family). The ip
+  configs are present while connected, as `{method: "static", address, prefix, gateway, dns1,
+  dns2, mtu}` (`method` is `static` for raw-ip, `dhcp` for a driver in 802.3 mode, then without the
+  addresses). uqmid does not configure the interface or the routes: that is the job of the netifd protocol.
+  Deviation: `stats` has the duration only (no rx/tx bytes).
+* `connect`: reply `{...get...}` or the error with `connection_error` (MM's `ConnectionError`).
+  The errors are `unauthorized` (roaming and not allowed), `in-progress` (already connecting),
+  `wrong-state` (the modem is not enabled), `not-found` (no data port), `unsupported` (no WDS),
+  `failed` and `aborted` (a disconnect cancelled it).
+* `disconnect`: reply `{}`. A connect in progress is cancelled (the connect fails with `aborted`).
+
+The connect does not wait for the registration (MM neither: only roaming forbids it; `simple_connect` registers first).
+
+Connect sequence (mm-bearer-qmi.c `connect_context_step()`):
+
+1. the first net port which is not in use is the data port (net port with the lowest `dev_port`; the
+   first bearer uses `dev_port` 0). Without any net port the connect fails (`not-found`).
+2. data format (mm-port-qmi.c `mm_port_qmi_setup_data_format()`): WDA get data format; if it is raw-ip without aggregation
+   nothing is set, otherwise WDA set data format (raw-ip, no aggregation). If WDA is not available or
+   fails, CTL set data format (raw-ip, no QoS header) is used, like MM's reopening of the port with the
+   raw-ip flag. CTL is not sent if WDA worked (MM neither: a repeated CTL request is rejected with 70). If
+   neither works and the netdev is a bam-dmux one the connect fails. For a qmi_wwan netdev raw_ip of the driver is enabled (with the
+   interface down).
+3. for each IP family (`ipv4`; `ipv6`; `ipv4v6`: two WDS clients, IPv4 first): allocate a WDS client, WDS
+   bind data port (bam-dmux: SIO port `A2_MUX_RMNET<dev_port>`, qcom-soc plugin; if the modem answers
+   DEVICE_UNSUPPORTED the client is used unbound: MM's bind mux data port fallback is not implemented),
+   set IP family, register for the packet service status indication, start network (profile index, or
+   APN, authentication preference, user, password; 60 s timeout) and get the current settings.
+   NoEffect of start network is a connected network, like MM.
+4. the bearer is connected if at least one family connected (MM: "If one of IPv4 or IPv6 succeeds,
+   we're connected"). The error of the other family is only logged; the family has no config. If both fail
+   the error of IPv4 is `connection_error`. Examples: `pdp-type-ipv4-only-allowed` (3GPP cause 50: an
+   IPv6 PDN is rejected, as with LG U+).
+   Deviation: MM sets the WDS event report; it has no use here and is not done.
+5. `connected` -> the modem state is `connecting` -> `connected` (while another bearer is connected
+   it stays `connected`).
+
+Disconnect (`disconnect_context_step()`): stop network of each family, then the WDS clients (client ids) are
+released. A failing stop network leaves the bearer `connected` (error to the caller); the forced
+disconnections ignore errors.
+
+Forced disconnection (mm-base-bearer.c): the packet service status indication `disconnected` of a
+client disconnects the whole bearer (the other family too) with the call end reason as `connection_error`.
+The registration is watched like MM: registered in a roaming network with `allow_roaming` false or
+emergency only disconnects at once, an unregistered modem after 15 s (a bearer which is not connected yet
+is cancelled at once).
+
+Voice interface
+---------------
+
+Methods of the modem object (MM: Modem.Voice). Calls need the QMI VOICE service. The VOICE client is allocated
+when the modem is initialized and kept until the modem is removed (MM: `qmi_services[]` and
+`mm_shared_qmi_ensure_client()` in `mm-broadband-modem-qmi.c`). Enabling registers the call and supplementary
+service notification events (`common_voice_enable_disable_unsolicited_events()`); disabling only unregisters the
+indications without releasing the client, matching `mm_iface_modem_voice_disable()`. If the service or indication registration is not
+available, voice methods fail with `wrong-state`, but modem enable still works.
+
+* `voice_list_calls` -> `{"calls": [path, ...]}` (MM: `ListCalls`)
+* `voice_create_call {number}` -> `{"path"}` (MM: `CreateCall`): creates an outgoing call in the state
+  `unknown`; it is dialed by its `start` method. Needs an enabled modem (the VOICE client persists, but call
+  indications are registered only while enabled) and a number (`invalid-args`).
+* `voice_delete_call {path}` (MM: `DeleteCall`): removes the call object, `not-found` for an unknown path.
+  Deviation: a call which is not terminated is hung up first (best effort, End Call without waiting for the
+  answer), MM just drops the object.
+* `voice_hangup_all` (MM: `HangupAll`): Manage Calls "end all calls". On success the calls which are
+  dialing, ringing or active are `terminated` (reason `terminated`); waiting and held calls follow the reports.
+* `voice_hold_and_accept` (MM: `HoldAndAccept`): Manage Calls "hold active, accept waiting or held". The
+  active calls become `held`, the waiting (else the first held) call `active` (reason `accepted`).
+* `voice_hangup_and_accept` (MM: `HangupAndAccept`): Manage Calls "release active, accept waiting or held".
+  The active calls are `terminated`, the waiting (else the first held) call `active` (reason `accepted`).
+* `voice_transfer` (MM: `Transfer`): Manage Calls "explicit call transfer"; the active and held calls are
+  `terminated` (reason `transferred`).
+* `voice_call_waiting_setup {enable: bool}` (MM: `CallWaitingSetup`): Set Supplementary Service (call
+  waiting activate/deactivate).
+* `voice_call_waiting_query` -> `{"status": bool}` (MM: `CallWaitingQuery`): Get Call Waiting, `true` if the
+  voice class is active.
+
+Multiparty calls (join/leave) are not supported, `multiparty` is always `false`.
+
+Call `uqmid.modem.<N>.call.<M>` (MM: Call):
+
+* `get` -> `{path, state, state_reason, direction, number, multiparty, qmi_call_id, end_reason?, cc_cause?, audio_port, audio_format}`
+  * `state`: `unknown`, `dialing`, `ringing-out`, `ringing-in`, `active`, `held`, `waiting`, `terminated`
+  * `state_reason`: `unknown`, `outgoing-started`, `incoming-new`, `accepted`, `terminated`, `refused-or-busy`,
+    `error`, `audio-setup-failed`, `transferred`, `deflected`
+  * `direction`: `unknown`, `incoming`, `outgoing`
+  * `qmi_call_id` is an uqmid addition: the call id of the QMI VOICE service (the id of `uqmi --voice-*`), 0 while the call has none
+  * `end_reason` and `cc_cause` are uqmid additions, see "Call end reasons" below (only present once known)
+  * `audio_port` is `""` and `audio_format` `{}`: the audio of a call is a separate ALSA card and is not handled
+    by uqmid
+* `start` (MM: `Start`): dials a call in the state `unknown` (`failed` otherwise). A number which is not an
+  emergency number (112, 911, and with no SIM 000, 08, 110, 999, 118, 119) is refused (`unauthorized`, the call is
+  `terminated`) while `emergency_only` is `true`. The call becomes `dialing` (reason `outgoing-started`), QMI
+  Dial Call (90 s timeout) gives its call id. The following states come from the call status indications
+  (`ringing-out`, `active`, ...). If the dialing fails the call is `terminated` (reason `unknown`) and the
+  request returns an error.
+* `accept` (MM: `Accept`): answers a call in the state `ringing-in` (`failed` otherwise), the call becomes `active`
+  (reason `accepted`). On a failure the call is `terminated` (reason `error`).
+* `deflect {number}` (MM: `Deflect`): `unsupported` (MM: the QMI call class has no deflect either).
+* `hangup` (MM: `Hangup`): ends a call which is not `unknown` or `terminated` (`failed` otherwise). The call
+  is `terminated` (reason `terminated`) even if the request failed.
+* `send_dtmf {dtmf}` (MM: `SendDtmf`): only for an `active` call. For each character (`0-9 * # A-D`) QMI Start
+  Continuous DTMF is sent, 500 ms later Stop Continuous DTMF, one after the other. The reply comes when
+  the last character is done. (MM sends only the first character.)
+
+Notifications of the call object: `state_changed {old, new, reason, end_reason?, cc_cause?}` (`end_reason` and `cc_cause` only when `new` is
+`terminated` and known, uqmid additions). `dtmf_received` is not emitted: QMI
+has no such indication in MM either (only the AT based modems report received DTMF).
+
+How the call list follows the modem (mm_iface_modem_voice_report_all_calls()): the All Call Status
+indication (all calls, with state, direction and remote number) is matched with the known calls by
+direction+state, number or call id (the call id of a call is the one of the Dial Call answer or of the first
+report). A call which is not in the report is `terminated` (reason `unknown`), the call id is reset then. A
+reported incoming call (state `ringing-in` or `waiting`) which is unknown creates a new call object (reason
+`incoming-new`) and `voice.call_added`. QMI state mapping: origination and cc-in-progress are `dialing`,
+alerting `ringing-out`, setup and incoming `ringing-in`, conversation `active`, hold `held`, waiting
+`waiting`, disconnecting and end `terminated`. There is no incoming call timeout and no polling (like MM
+with QMI). The Supplementary Service indication marks a call `held` (call is on hold), `active` (call is
+retrieved) or `waiting` (reason `refused-or-busy`, outgoing call is waiting). Terminated calls stay in the
+list until `voice_delete_call`.
+
+Deviations: an entry of the report without a Remote Party Number is reported without number (MM drops it,
+which terminates the known call); the EF_ECC numbers of the SIM are not read (with a SIM only 112 and 911
+are emergency numbers).
+
+Messaging interface
+-------------------
+
+Methods of the modem object (MM: Modem.Messaging) and the sms objects `uqmid.modem.<N>.sms.<M>` (MM: Sms). SMS
+need the QMI WMS service (3GPP point to point messages; CDMA and cell broadcast messages are ignored). The WMS
+client is allocated when the modem is initialized and kept until the modem is removed (MM: `qmi_services[]` in
+`mm-broadband-modem-qmi.c`).
+
+Enabling (MM: `mm_iface_modem_messaging_enable()`, never fails the enable of the modem):
+
+1. the default storage: Get Routes (5 s), the storage of the class 0/1 route with the action "store and notify";
+   if the modem does not tell, `me` (MM: `get_best_initial_default_sms_storage()`). Then Set Routes for class 0
+   and 1 (point to point, store and notify) with the default storage (a failure is only a warning). The
+   default storage is only read from the modem the first time; after that the one chosen with
+   `messaging_set_default_storage` is kept (also over a disable/enable) and set again, like MM keeps its
+   default storage. Deviations: MM does the Get Routes when it initializes the modem, here it is the first
+   enable; uqmid does a Get Routes at every enable and again after the Set Routes and logs the whole routing
+   table at `info` level ("SMS routes (before)" / "SMS routes (after)": type, class, storage and receipt action
+   of every entry). This shows how the modem routes the classes MM does not touch (2, 3, none): an entry with the
+   action `transfer-only` / `transfer-and-ack` is not stored, such a message only arrives in the Event Report.
+2. the initial SMS of the storages `sm` and `me` (MM: `load_initial_sms_parts()`): List Messages (5 s) without a
+   tag; on a QMI error one list per tag (MT read, MT not read, MO sent, MO not sent, 3 s each); a Raw Read (3 s,
+   errors are ignored) per message. A message which was already stored is listed with the state `received`
+   (MT), `sent` (MO sent) or `stored` (MO not sent). Deviation: a List Messages which times out does not end the
+   storage (MM gives the storage up). The LG U+ UZ801 (MSM8916 modem) has no list without a tag and never
+   answers the one for MT read: that costs one 3 s timeout per storage and the next tag is tried (after 3
+   timeouts of a storage it is given up). If the WMS client id can not be allocated the step ends and the SMS
+   interface is not available.
+3. the event report: the indication is registered and the "new MT message indicator" is switched on (errors are
+   ignored).
+
+Disabling switches the indicator off, keeps the WMS client allocated and removes the SMS objects (MM clears the
+SMS list at this point, there is no `messaging.deleted`). Requests of the user which wait for the modem are failed
+with `aborted`.
+
+New messages: the Event Report indication names the storage and the index of a message which was stored: it is
+read with Raw Read (3 s) and added. A transfer-route message (not stored) is acknowledged (Send Ack) if the modem
+wants that and added directly (`storage` `unknown`). Like MM the data of a transfer-route message is a TPDU
+without the SMSC field. Deviations: the data is copied before the acknowledgement is built (the QMI parser
+keeps it in a static buffer which every request encoder overwrites; a TPDU which was parsed after the Send Ack
+was garbage, `Couldn't read address: field missing`); a firmware quirk fallback decodes data which fails as a
+TPDU once more as a full PDU with an SMSC field (`00` + TPDU is also accepted); if a transfer-route message
+can not be parsed at all (it is lost: acknowledged and not stored) the raw TLV 0x11 is logged at `notice`
+level: `ack_indicator`, `transaction_id`, `format` (0x06 3GPP point to point, 0x07 broadcast, 0x08 MWI, 0x00
+CDMA), `length` and the data as hex (up to 255 bytes).
+
+Storing the messages on the SIM: the routes decide where an incoming message goes. MM's default storage is the
+one of the modem (`default_storage`, usually `me` or what the carrier configuration sets), uqmid sets the same
+routes (class 0 and 1). To get the messages on the SIM, like with MM's `SetDefaultStorage`, call
+`messaging_set_default_storage {"storage": "sm"}` after the modem is enabled; the routes are set (and logged
+before and after) and the choice stays over a disable/enable. A message which can not be stored (for example
+the SIM is full or the class is routed to transfer) arrives as a transfer-route message and is only kept if it
+parses.
+
+* `messaging_list` -> `{"messages": [path, ...]}` (MM: `List`). Needs the state `enabled` or above
+  (`wrong-state` otherwise, also for `messaging_create`).
+* `messaging_create {number, text | data, smsc?, validity?, class?, delivery_report_request?}` -> `{"path"}`
+  (MM: `Create`): creates an sms object in the state `unknown` (not sent, not stored) and emits `messaging.added`
+  with `received: false`. `text` is UTF-8, `data` a hex string (binary message), only one of them. `smsc` is the
+  address of the service center (default: the one of the SIM), `validity` the relative validity period in
+  minutes (up to 635040, 0: the default of the network), `class` 0 to 3 (default: none), `delivery_report_request`
+  asks for a status report. `invalid-args` for a missing or wrong parameter; deviation: MM checks the number and
+  the text when it creates the PDUs (send, store), uqmid checks them here (number: `+` and up to 20 digits
+  `0-9 * # a b c`; text not empty and valid UTF-8).
+* `messaging_delete {path}` (MM: `Delete`): deletes all parts of the message from the storage (WMS Delete, 5 s
+  each), then removes the object and emits `messaging.deleted`. `not-found` for an unknown path. Failing parts
+  give `failed` "Couldn't delete N parts from this SMS" and the object stays. Deleting is allowed while the modem
+  is enabling or disabling (the messages are added before it is `enabled`), `wrong-state` below that.
+* `messaging_set_default_storage {storage: "sm"|"me"}` (MM: `SetDefaultStorage`): Set Routes (class 0 and 1,
+  store and notify) with the storage and the `default_storage` property follows. `unsupported` for another
+  storage. The routes are logged before and after (see above). The reply is `{"state", "power_state"}` like the
+  other operations (MM's `SetDefaultStorage` has no output).
+
+Sms `uqmid.modem.<N>.sms.<M>`:
+
+* `get` -> the properties (MM names in snake_case):
+
+  | key | value |
+  |---|---|
+  | `path` | name of the object |
+  | `state` | `unknown`, `stored`, `receiving`, `received`, `sending` (never used, like in MM), `sent` |
+  | `pdu_type` | `unknown`, `deliver` (received), `submit` (to send), `status-report` |
+  | `number` | the sender (deliver), the destination (submit) or the recipient (status report), `+` for an international number, text for an alphanumeric sender |
+  | `smsc` | the service center, `""` if the PDU has none |
+  | `text` | UTF-8 text (GSM 7 bit incl. the extension table and UTF-16 are decoded), absent if the message has none |
+  | `data` | hex string of binary (8 bit) messages, absent if the message has none. Text and data are never both there for a normal message |
+  | `validity_type`, `validity` | `relative` and the minutes, or `unknown` and 0 |
+  | `class` | 0 to 3, -1 if not used |
+  | `delivery_report_request` | bool |
+  | `message_reference` | of the sent message (the last part), the one of a status report |
+  | `timestamp`, `discharge_timestamp` | ISO8601 `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS+HH:MM` (service center time stamp, discharge time of a status report), absent if the PDU has none |
+  | `delivery_state` | number, the TP-STATUS of a status report (0 completed, 0x100: unknown, MM `DeliveryState`) |
+  | `storage` | `unknown` (not stored or transfer-route), `sm` or `me` |
+  | `multipart`, `max_parts`, `received_parts` | uqmid additions: a multipart message and its parts |
+  | `indices` | uqmid addition: the storage indices (WMS memory index) of the parts which are stored in `sm` / `me`, empty if not stored |
+
+  A multipart message is one object. The parts are matched by concat reference, number and number of parts (and
+  pdu type). The object is listed (`messaging.added` with `received: true`) when the first part arrives, in the
+  state `receiving`; `text` and `data` are filled when the last part arrived and the state becomes `received`
+  (`state_changed`). A message which never completes stays listed until it is deleted (which deletes the parts
+  which arrived).
+* `send` (MM: `Send`): sends the message. `failed` for a received message ("This SMS was received, cannot send
+  it") and for one which was already sent. The text is split like MM: GSM 7 bit if all characters can be coded
+  (single part up to 160 septets, 153 per part of a multipart message), UTF-16 otherwise (single part up to 70
+  characters / 140 bytes, 67 characters / 134 bytes per part); binary data 140 bytes, 134 per part. A multipart
+  message gets a random concat reference 1..254. Every part is sent with Raw Send (300 s timeout); a message which
+  was stored is sent from the storage (Send From Memory Storage, on "invalid QMI command" with Raw Send). On
+  success the state is `sent` and `message_reference` is the one of the last part. An error of a part ends the
+  send (`failed` with the QMI error, the RP/TP cause is logged); parts which were sent stay sent. A message with
+  more than 255 parts is `invalid-args`.
+* `store {storage?}` (MM: `Store`): writes the parts to `sm` or `me` (default: the default storage) with Raw
+  Write (5 s each). Stored in another storage: `failed`; already in that storage: success. The multipart
+  concat reference is unique among the stored messages for the same number. The state becomes `stored`,
+  `storage` is set. Deviation: parts which were written before a failure are deleted again.
+
+Notifications of the sms object: `state_changed {old, new}`.
+
+Other deviations: a send, store or delete of an sms which another one of the same sms runs is refused
+(`in-progress`); a received part without user data is an empty text (MM drops it); an invalid UTF-16 sequence
+is replaced by U+FFFD; at most 512 sms objects are listed.
+
+Simple interface
+----------------
+
+* `simple_connect {pin?, operator_id?, apn, ip_type?, allowed_auth?, user?, password?, allow_roaming?, profile_id?}`
+  (MM: `Simple.Connect`, `connection_step()`): unlock check (the SIM PIN is sent if the modem is locked and
+  a `pin` was given; other locks: `unauthorized`), wait to get initialized, `enable`, wait for the
+  final state, 100 ms, register (60 s), create the bearer or use the one with the same properties, connect.
+  Reply `{bearer: <path>}`; on a failed connect `{error, message, connection_error?}`. Only one simple connect at a
+  time (`in-progress`). Deviation: MM's wait for the packet service state `attached` is not done.
+* `simple_disconnect {bearer?}` (MM: `Simple.Disconnect`): aborts a running `simple_connect` (it fails with
+  `aborted`) and disconnects all bearers or the given one. `{}`.
+* `simple_get_status` (MM: `Simple.GetStatus`): `{state, signal_quality: {value, recent}, access_technologies,
+  m3gpp_registration_state, m3gpp_operator_code, m3gpp_operator_name, bearers}`.
+
+SIM `uqmid.modem.<N>.sim.<M>`
+-----------------------------
+
+The object is created when the card is known to be present (also if it is
+locked), like MM's SIM object. It disappears together with the modem object
+(see SIM hot swap). The state changing methods are serialized with the other
+requests of the modem (MM's operation lock) and reply with
+`{"state", "power_state"}` of the modem.
+
+`get`:
+
+| key | value (MM property) |
+|---|---|
+| `path`, `index` | ubus object name and its index |
+| `active` | `Active`, always `true` (the object is the active SIM) |
+| `sim_identifier` | `SimIdentifier`, the ICCID (EF_ICCID, from the slot status or read from the card) |
+| `imsi` | `Imsi` (empty while the SIM is locked) |
+| `eid` | `Eid`, empty if it is not an eUICC |
+| `operator_identifier` | `OperatorIdentifier`: MCC and MNC of the IMSI, the MNC length is taken from EF_AD like MM. Deviation: if there is no EF_AD or it has no MNC length (many cards have a 3 byte EF_AD) the MCC decides: the MCCs with 3 digit MNCs (North America, India, ...) are known, everything else (e.g. 450/Korea) has 2 digits. MM leaves the identifier empty then |
+| `operator_name` | `OperatorName`: the SPN (EF_SPN), empty if it can not be read |
+| `sim_type` | `SimType`: `physical` (`esim` for an eUICC) |
+| `removability` | `Removability`: `unknown` (MM does not load it for QMI) |
+
+Methods (MM errors, as `{"error", "message"}` with ubus status
+`PERMISSION_DENIED` for the lock related ones):
+
+* `send_pin {pin}` (`SendPin`): only if the modem is locked by the SIM PIN,
+  otherwise `wrong-state`. On success the lock info is updated (the sim fsm
+  runs again, MM: `update_lock_info`) and the modem goes `locked` ->
+  `disabled`. A wrong PIN is `incorrect-password` (the message has the retries
+  left), a blocked PIN is `sim-puk` and the modem is reprobed (MM:
+  `reprobe_if_puk_discovered`). uqmid never unlocks by itself.
+* `send_puk {puk, pin}` (`SendPuk`): only if locked by the SIM PUK
+  (`wrong-state` otherwise); `pin` is the new PIN. A permanently blocked or
+  missing SIM is `sim-failure` and the modem is reprobed.
+* `enable_pin {pin, enabled}` (`EnablePin`) and `change_pin {old, new}`
+  (`ChangePin`): errors as above, the retries are updated afterwards.
+
+All of them use the primary GW provisioning session and PIN1 (UPIN if the card
+status says so).
+
+SIM hot swap
+------------
+
+Like MM (mm-shared-qmi.c, mm-iface-modem.c) a SIM event disables the modem
+(if it is enabled), then the modem object is invalidated: `modem.removed` is
+sent, the object (and its sim object) goes away and the device is probed again,
+`modem.added` follows with a new index. The modem is NOT reset. Whoever has
+enabled the modem has to enable the new one.
+
+Events: UIM refresh of the types reset and init-full-fcn (when it ended, or 3 s
+after it started; other refreshes are only completed), a change of an active
+slot in the UIM slot status indication (card removed or inserted, ICCID
+changed, slot switched) and, after a refresh, a check of presence, ICCID and
+IMSI of the SIM against what was seen before (`SIM removed`, `SIM inserted`,
+`new SIM detected`, `SIM not changed` in the log).
+
+Deviation: modems without slot status support (the SoC modem of the MSM8916)
+would never see a removed or inserted card with MM's method. For them the UIM
+card status event (UIM Register Events, card status flag) starts the same
+check. The check waits until the modem has finished its initialization.
+
+Carrier configuration
+---------------------
+
+If the PDC service exists, the modem reads the selected SOFTWARE configuration
+during the initialization (PDC Get Selected Config, then Get Config Info for
+the description and version; both answers are indications). Nothing is selected
+or loaded, uqmid only shows what the modem uses. The requests are small (the
+SMD transport of the SoC modem drops QMUX messages of 1 KiB and more). A
+failure leaves the properties empty and does not fail the initialization.
+
+PDC (uqmid extension)
+---------------------
+
+ModemManager has no public PDC methods (it only has the read only
+`CarrierConfiguration` properties). On user request uqmid adds methods to the
+modem object to manage the modem configurations (MCFG/MBN files). **These are
+NV writing operator actions: they change the configuration of the modem
+persistently and are never run automatically by uqmid.** A wrong
+configuration can leave the modem without service.
+
+All methods take `type` (`software`, the default, or `platform`) and are user
+requests serialized per modem like `enable`:
+
+* `pdc_list {type}` -> `{type, configs: [{id, description, version, total_size, active, selected, pending}]}`.
+  `id` is the hex configuration id, `version` is `%08X`. `active` is the
+  configuration the modem runs, `selected` the one which is going to run (the
+  pending one after `pdc_activate` and before the modem restarted, else the
+  active one), `pending` is only the pending one. An empty list is returned
+  if the modem sends no indication 10 s after the request (a modem without
+  stored configuration is silent).
+* `pdc_selected {type}` -> `{type, selected, active, pending}`. A key is left
+  out if there is no such configuration.
+* `pdc_load {file, type}` -> `{type, id, size}`. Reads the MBN file of the
+  local filesystem and sends it in chunks of 512 bytes (the SMD transport of
+  the SoC modem drops QMUX messages of 1 KiB and more). The modem confirms each
+  chunk with an indication (10 s each, a timeout, a frame reset or an error
+  fails the request). The id of the configuration is the SHA1 of the whole file
+  (like qmicli and `uqmi --pdc-load-config`). Progress is logged at `debug`
+  level. Files are limited to 16 MiB.
+* `pdc_activate {id, type}` -> `{type, id, status: "activation-requested"}`.
+  Like MM (`mm_shared_qmi_set_carrier_config()`): Set Selected Config, then
+  Activate Config. The modem restarts to apply the configuration: the request
+  succeeds once the activation was accepted, once no indication came for 10 s
+  or if the modem vanishes afterwards (the removal is handled by hotplug and
+  the carrier configuration is read when it is probed again).
+* `pdc_deactivate {type}` -> `{type, status: "deactivation-requested"}`.
+* `pdc_delete {id, type}` -> `{type, id}`.
+
+States: all methods need the modem to be at least `disabled` (error
+`wrong-state` otherwise, `unsupported` without a PDC service). `pdc_load`,
+`pdc_activate`, `pdc_deactivate` and `pdc_delete` additionally fail with
+`wrong-state` while a data connection is `connecting`, `connected` or
+`disconnecting`. After `pdc_load`, `pdc_delete` the `carrier_configuration` and
+`carrier_configuration_revision` properties are read again.
+
+The same operations are available in the command line tool as
+`uqmi --pdc-list-configs` and friends.
+
+uqmid extensions (not in ModemManager)
+--------------------------------------
+
+### Call end reasons
+
+ModemManager ignores the call end reason of the QMI All Call Status indication: a call which the indication ends
+or no longer lists is `terminated` with the `state_reason` `unknown` (`terminated` after a hangup request of the
+user). uqmid keeps exactly that `state_reason` and adds the QMI detail as the `end_reason` property of the call
+object (`get`), and in the `state_changed` notification of the call object and the `voice.call_state_changed`
+notification of the modem when `new` is `terminated`:
+
+    "end_reason": {"value": 146, "name": "user-busy"}
+
+`value` is the QMI VOICE call end reason (`QmiVoiceCallEndReason` of libqmi, the 16 bit value of the "Call End
+Reason" TLV 0x14 of the All Call Status indication, which libqmi does not decode), `name` its libqmi nick
+(`unknown` for a value which is not in the table). The property is absent until a reason is known. Sources:
+
+* the Call End Reason TLV (0x14) of the All Call Status indication (the reason is taken when it arrives, also if the call
+  is terminated by a later report which does not list it any more);
+* a hangup requested through uqmid (`hangup`, `voice_hangup_all`, `voice_hangup_and_accept`, `voice_transfer`, which
+  terminate the call with the `state_reason` `terminated` or `transferred`) sets `client-end` (29) if no reason came
+  from the modem before. The reason of the modem for such a call is not seen: the call is already terminated.
+
+Examples (a client such as a SIP bridge can map them): remote party busy `{"value": 146, "name": "user-busy"}`
+(`unassigned-number` 141, `call-barred` 115, `operator-determined-barring` 144, `rejected-by-network` 135,
+`user-alerting-no-answer` 148, `no-user-responding` 147, `call-rejected` 149) for an outgoing call; the remote party
+hanging up `normal-call-clearing` (145) or `network-end` (104); a local hangup `client-end` (29). Which value the
+modem reports for which situation depends on the modem and the network.
+
+`cc_cause` (u32, only present when the modem sent it for the call) is the raw call end cause value of Annex H of
+3GPP TS 24.008 (e.g. 17 user busy, 26 non-selected user clearing, 31 normal unspecified), from the "Raw Call End
+Cause Code" TLV 0x35 of the All Call Status indication (an array of call id and cause byte). Like the Call End
+Reason TLV it is not decoded by libqmi; both are taken from the Qualcomm QMI VOICE IDL (voice_service_v02:
+`call_end_reason` 0x14 and `raw_call_end_cause_code` 0x35 of `voice_all_call_status_ind_msg`). Older firmware may
+not send TLV 0x35 (or 0x14), then the property is absent. A call hung up by a request of the user has no
+`cc_cause` unless the modem reported one before. Example of a terminated notification:
+
+    "end_reason": {"value": 146, "name": "user-busy"}, "cc_cause": 17
+
+The Get All Call Info response (`uqmi --voice-get-all-call-info`, which lists only calls which are not ended) has no such TLV
+in libqmi and is unchanged, as is the output of the command line tool.
+
+Everything below is a **uqmid extension**: an explicit exception to the rule
+that the methods follow ModemManager. ModemManager has no public method for
+these QMI features, so uqmid offers its own ("uqmid dialect"), one method per
+`uqmi` command, so that every command of the command line tool has a ubus
+counterpart. The methods are on the modem object (`uqmid.modem.<N>`); each sends
+the QMI request of the command (see `uqmi/commands-*.c`) and returns the JSON
+the command prints (the formatting code is shared with the command line tool).
+
+**Warning:** the state-changing extensions (`uim_power_*`, `uim_channel_*`,
+`uim_apdu_send`, `*_data_format`, `nas_set_network_roaming`,
+`wds_set_autoconnect`) change the modem behind the back of uqmid. uqmid does not
+re-read the state afterwards, so its ModemManager style model (SIM and PIN
+state, data format of the bearers, roaming flag, registration) can become stale
+until the modem is disabled and enabled again or the device is probed again
+(`reset`, or the removal and the next kernel event). Use them for diagnosis and
+setup, not while another user of the modem relies on the model.
+
+Common rules:
+
+* The reads (`dms_get_versions`, `nas_get_*`, `uim_get_sim_state`,
+  `wda_get_data_format`, `wms_get_raw_message`) are sent at once, they do not
+  take the operation lock. The other methods are user requests serialized per
+  modem like `enable` (the reply carries `state` and `power_state` and, where
+  noted, the data of the command).
+* There is no state requirement, except where QMI needs one: the data format
+  can't be changed during a data call, so `wda_set_data_format` and
+  `ctl_set_data_format` fail with `wrong-state` while the modem is `connecting`,
+  `connected` or `disconnecting`. All methods fail with `wrong-state` while the
+  modem was not probed yet (the services are not known) and with `unsupported`
+  if the modem has no such service. A QMI error (or a timeout after 10 s,
+  30 s for the UIM changes) fails the request with `failed` (`timeout`) and
+  the QMI error in the message.
+* Arguments which are numbers are JSON numbers; a bad argument is
+  `invalid-args`.
+
+Reads:
+
+* `dms_get_versions` -> `{service_<id>: "<major>,<minor>"}` (`uqmi --get-versions`; CTL Get Version Info).
+* `nas_get_tx_rx_info {radio}` -> `{rx_chain_<0-3>: {tuned, rssi, ...}, tx: {traffic, tx_power}}`. `radio` is `gsm`, `umts`, `lte` or `5gnr` (`--get-tx-rx-info`).
+* `nas_get_lte_cphy_ca_info` -> `{primary: {cell_id, channel, band, ...}, secondary[_<n>]: {..., state}}` (`--get-lte-cphy-ca-info`).
+* `uim_get_sim_state` -> `{card_slot, card_application_state, pin1_status, pin1_verify_tries, pin1_unlock_tries, pin2_*}` of the first present card (`--uim-get-sim-state`; empty if there is none).
+* `nas_get_network_roaming` -> `{roaming}`: `any`, `off`, `only`, `not-flashing` or `unknown` (`--get-network-roaming`, which prints the bare string).
+* `wda_get_data_format` -> `{qos-format, link-layer-protocol, data-aggregation-protocol, uplink-data-aggregation-max-datagrams, ..., flow-control}` (`--wda-get-data-format`).
+* `wms_get_raw_message {storage, index}` -> `{data}`: the raw message as lowercase hex bytes separated by a space (`--get-raw-message`, which prints the bare string). `storage` is `sim` (default) or `me`.
+
+Changes:
+
+* `uim_power_off {slot}`, `uim_power_on {slot}` (`--uim-power-off` / `--uim-power-on` with `--uim-slot`). `slot` is 1 or 2 and required.
+* `uim_channel_open {aid, slot}` -> `{channel_id, sw1, sw2}`; `aid` is hex (up to 16 bytes). `uim_channel_close {channel_id, slot}` (1-4). `uim_apdu_send {apdu, channel_id, slot}` -> `{response}` (hex; `apdu` up to 512 bytes; without `channel_id` the TLV is not sent, the modem decides). `slot` defaults to the active slot (1 if unknown).
+* `nas_set_network_roaming {roaming, duration}`: `roaming` is `any`, `off` (home networks only) or `only` (roaming networks only), `duration` (optional) `permanent` or `power-cycle` (`--set-network-roaming` with `--network-change-duration`; NAS Set System Selection Preference). This is the modem's own preference, not the `allow_roaming` of the bearer.
+* `wda_set_data_format {type, dl_aggregation_protocol, dl_datagram_max_count, dl_datagram_max_size, ul_aggregation_protocol, ul_datagram_max_count, ul_datagram_max_size, flow_control}`: `type` is `802.3` or `raw-ip` (required), the protocols are `tlp`, `qc-cm`, `mbim`, `rndis`, `qmap` or `qmapv5`, `flow_control` is 0 or 1; not given means disabled/0 like the options of `--wda-set-data-format`.
+* `ctl_set_data_format {mode}`: `802.3` or `raw-ip` (`--set-data-format`; CTL Set Data Format without QoS header).
+* `wds_set_autoconnect {status}`: `disabled`, `enabled` or `paused` (`--set-autoconnect`; WDS Set Autoconnect Settings). With autoconnect enabled the modem dials by itself, uqmid does not know about that connection.
+* `voice_burst_dtmf {call_id, digits}`: VOICE Burst DTMF (`--voice-burst-dtmf`). CDMA only: other modems answer with an error, which is passed on. For GSM/UMTS/LTE calls use `send_dtmf` of the call object.
+* `dms_fcc_auth`: DMS Set FCC Authentication (`--fcc-auth`).
+
+PDC (see above): `pdc_list`, `pdc_selected`, `pdc_load`, `pdc_activate`,
+`pdc_deactivate`, `pdc_delete`.
+
+Not available via uqmid: the commands which would conflict with state uqmid owns
+have no method. `--bind-data-port` (uqmid binds the data ports of its bearers),
+`--reset-wds` (uqmid owns the WDS clients), `--sync` (uqmid handles CTL sync) and
+`--get-client-id` and `--set-client-id` (uqmid owns the client
+ids of its services).
+
+uqmi --uqmid (command line client of uqmid)
+--------------------------------------------
+
+`uqmi --uqmid` (short `-u`) runs the commands of the `uqmi` command line tool through uqmid instead of opening the
+device (which uqmid owns): uqmi does not open anything, it calls the methods above (ubus, `libubus`) and prints the
+same JSON the direct mode prints. The direct mode (without `--uqmid`) is not changed.
+
+    uqmi --uqmid --get-imei
+    uqmi --uqmid -d /dev/wwan0qmi0 --get-serving-system
+    uqmi --uqmid -d wwan0qmi0 --apn internet --start-network
+
+* `-d`, `--device` selects the modem like the `device` option of the netifd protocol: the `/dev` node, the port
+  name (`wwan0qmi0`, `cdc-wdm0`) or the physdev, see `uqmid list`. Without `-d` the only modem is used (an error if
+  there are none or several).
+* `-t <msecs>` is the timeout of one ubus call (default 10 minutes: a scan or a message send takes up to 300 s) and
+  ends `--voice-indications`. `-s` prints single lines.
+* Options and commands run in the order of the direct mode (the options first, then the commands in the order of the
+  command line); the first failure ends the run with the exit code 255 (`-1`) like in the direct mode, exit code 2
+  if uqmid or the modem is not found.
+* Errors: the `{error, message}` of uqmid (or the ubus status) is printed on **stdout** as a JSON string
+  (`"wrong-state: modem in disabled state"`), like the errors of the direct mode.
+* Not available: the commands which would conflict with the state uqmid owns (`--sync`, `--get-client-id`,
+  `--set-client-id`, `-k` / `--keep-client-id`, `-r` / `--release-client-id`, `--bind-data-port`, `--bind-mux` with
+  `--endpoint-type` / `--endpoint-iface`, `--reset-wds`, `--set-ip-family`, `--lte-attach-pdn`, `-m`) and those with
+  no uqmid method (`--set-default-profile`): they print `"--<name>: not available with --uqmid"` on stdout and
+  fail.
+
+### Mapping of the commands
+
+| uqmi command | uqmid call(s) | output / remarks |
+|---|---|---|
+| `--get-versions` | `dms_get_versions` | same |
+| `--get-imei` | modem `get` `imei` | same (bare string, nothing if unknown) |
+| `--get-imsi`, `--get-iccid` | sim `get` `imsi`, `sim_identifier` | same |
+| `--get-msisdn` | modem `get` `own_numbers[0]` | same |
+| `--get-pin-status` | `uim_get_sim_state` | same keys (`pin<n>_unlock_tries` is `pin<n>_unblock_tries`) |
+| `--verify-pin1`, `--uim-verify-pin1` | sim `send_pin` | PIN2 commands (`--verify-pin2`, `--uim-verify-pin2`, `--set-pin2-protection`, `--change-pin2`, `--unblock-pin2`) are not available (uqmid handles PIN1) |
+| `--set-pin1-protection` (`--pin`) | sim `enable_pin` | |
+| `--change-pin1` (`--pin`, `--new-pin`) | sim `change_pin` | |
+| `--unblock-pin1` (`--puk`, `--new-pin`) | sim `send_puk` | |
+| `--get-capabilities` | modem `get` `supported_capabilities` | `networks` and `sim` only (no channel rates, no data service) |
