+Further semantic differences of the client mode: uqmid keeps the state of the modem (enabled, registered, bearers), so
+the commands need the modem to be in the state its method requires (`wrong-state` otherwise; `--start-network` enables
+a disabled modem); a request is serialized with the other operations of uqmid; the commands change uqmid's model
+(see the warning of the extensions) instead of only the modem.
+
+State model and initialization
+------------------------------
+
+After the port was added the modem is `initializing`: CTL sync, versions of
+the services, the capabilities (NAS Get System Selection Preference, NAS Get Technology Preference, DMS Get
+Capabilities: MM's `CURRENT_CAPABILITIES` and `SUPPORTED_CAPABILITIES`), DMS model, manufacturer, revision and ids,
+the supported modes and bands, DMS get operating
+mode (read only: `online` is `on`; `low_power`, `persistent_low_power` and
+`mode_only_low_power` are `low`; `offline` and `shutting_down` are `off`), the current modes and bands and
+the SIM (the order of `interface_initialization_step()` of MM, see "Modes and bands"). The QMI DMS, NAS, UIM, WDS, WMS, VOICE, PDS, OMA, LOC, PDC, DSD and SAR clients supported by the modem
+are kept for its lifetime (MM: `qmi_services[]` in `mm-broadband-modem-qmi.c`); each bearer still has separate WDS
+clients per IP family. The initialization never changes the power state. Unexpected initialization failures get
+up to two retries after 2 seconds, then the modem goes to `failed` with reason `unknown`; SIM failures map to
+`sim-missing`, `sim-error` or the `locked` state. A failing capabilities step (DMS Get Capabilities fails, the modem
+has no current capabilities, no NAS or DMS client) fails the initialization at once (no retry, the other steps are not
+run): `failed` with reason `unknown-capabilities`. Afterwards the modem is
+
+* `locked` if the SIM requires a PIN or PUK (use `send_pin` / `send_puk` of the sim object),
+* `failed` with `state_failed_reason` `sim-missing` if there is no SIM
+  (`sim-error` if the SIM cannot be used),
+* `disabled` otherwise.
+
+Nothing enables a modem by itself: whoever wants to use it calls `enable`
+(like with MM, where the one who connects enables the modem).
+
+A request which gets no answer is failed after 10 seconds, so the state
+machines never hang. The modem object is removed when the control port
+disappears or the stream fails (`modem.removed`); it is added again by the
+next `add` kernel event.
+
+Logging
+-------
+
+uqmid logs to syslog (facility daemon) with the matching priorities: errors
+are `err`, state and power state transitions are `info`. The default level
+is `info`. `uqmid --log-level=err|warning|info|debug` (or `-d` for debug)
+selects the level; debug adds every QMI message and FSM transition (and the
+per request chatter: client ids, slot/card status, file reads). SIM events and
+reprobes are logged at `notice`. The init
+script passes `--log-level=$LOG_LEVEL`, taken from `option log_level` in
+`/etc/config/uqmid` (section `global`) or the `LOG_LEVEL` environment
+variable, default `INFO`.
+
+Running
+-------
+
+* `/etc/init.d/uqmid` starts `uqmid-wrapper` (procd, with respawn) and
+  `uqmid-monitor` like the init script of OpenWrt's ModemManager package.
+* `/etc/hotplug.d/wwan/25-uqmid` and `/etc/hotplug.d/usbmisc/25-uqmid`
+  cache the kernel events in `/var/run/uqmid/events.cache` and report them
+  with `ubus call uqmid report_kernel_event`. The wrapper reports all cached
+  events again each time the daemon (re)starts. If ubusd restarts while uqmid is running, libubus reconnects and
+  re-adds the manager and all modem/sub-objects registered in that context.
+* `uqmid-monitor` subscribes to the notifications and sets netifd interfaces
+  with `proto uqmid` and a matching `device` option (device node, port name
+  or physdev) available or unavailable.
+
+Internals
+---------
+
+* The baseline DMS, NAS, UIM, WDS (initial EPS bearer), WMS and VOICE clients (plus PDS, OMA, LOC, PDC, DSD and SAR
+  when present) are allocated after the QMI service list is loaded and kept until the modem is removed, like MM's
+  `qmi_services[]`. Enable/disable only registers and unregisters the relevant indications. A WDS client is also
+  allocated per IP family of a connected bearer and released when the family is disconnected. Services of the same
+  type are told apart by their client id (responses, and indications; the broadcast client id goes to all of them).
+  A client id which is allocated while nobody waits for it anymore (the service was closed in the meantime) is
+  released again at once.
+* The indication callbacks of a service may register or remove any indication (also the one which is
+  called or the following ones) while the indications are dispatched: removed entries are only marked
+  during the dispatch and freed afterwards.
